    <div class="page">
        <div class="header-blue">
            <h1>Suez University — Faculty of Human Medicine</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Comprehensive MCQ Bank: Antitubercular Pharmacology | Reviewed by: Mohamed Mohsen Abdelhady</div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part I: Disease Characterization &amp; Drug Classification</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">1. Which of the following statements correctly describes Tuberculosis (T.B) as presented in the reference material?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> It is a newly emerged viral pandemic primarily affecting western societies.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> It is recognized as one of the oldest and most common infectious diseases globally.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> It is a disease strictly restricted to waterborne transmission pathways.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It is a localized, non-communicable opportunistic superficial fungal disease.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">2. How is Mycobacterium tuberculosis primarily transmitted between individuals?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Via contaminated food products or direct blood transfusion lines.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Through vectors such as mosquitoes or ticks carrying the bacteria.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Via droplets from coughs or sneezes generated by an infected individual.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Through direct skin-to-skin surface contact or vector spores.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">3. First-line antitubercular medications are explicitly preferred over second-line therapies because they are:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Highly effective, demonstrate less toxicity, and are cheap.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Less effective but completely devoid of any organ-related toxicity.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Expensive but clear the infection within 24 hours of administration.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Synthesized locally to bypass the blood-brain barrier selectively.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">4. Which of the following clinical profiles specifies the general features of second-line anti-TB medications?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> High therapeutic efficacy, high cost, and low profile of toxic reactions.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Broad spectrum profile, highly cheap, and safe for pediatric patients.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Exclusively targeting extracellular bacteria with zero hepatic elimination.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Less effective, more toxic to organ systems, and expensive.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part II: First-Line Agents — Pharmacology of Isoniazid (INH)</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">5. Which pharmacological trait allows Isoniazid (INH) to achieve therapeutic levels within the central nervous system?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Its highly hydrophilic structure which permits active vesicular transport.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Its selective binding affinity to the choroid plexus epithelial linings.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Its lipophilic nature, allowing it to easily pass the blood-brain barrier.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Its reliance on biliary excretion which bypasses renal clearance mechanics.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">6. Which pathway represents the major metabolic transformation process of Isoniazid (INH) inside the body?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Glucuronidation within the loop of Henle.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Acetylation within the liver.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Microscopic oxidation inside the pulmonary alveolar spaces.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Hydrolysis by plasma esterases in peripheral capillaries.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">7. What is the precise mechanism of action (MOA) of Isoniazid (INH) after entering mycobacteria?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> It converts to an active form, binds enzymes, and inhibits mycolic acid synthesis only.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It attaches directly to DNA-dependent RNA polymerase to block transcription.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> It disrupts the 50S ribosomal subunit to arrest translation permanently.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It competitively displaces PABA inside the folic acid synthesis cycle.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">8. To mitigate peripheral neurotoxicity during Isoniazid (INH) treatment, which agent must be co-administered?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Para-amino-salicylic acid (PAS)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Vitamin C (Ascorbic acid)</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine)</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">9. Regarding drug interactions, Isoniazid (INH) behaves as a:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Potent hepatic microsomal enzyme inducer.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Hepatic microsomal enzyme inhibitor.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Neutral compound with zero impact on metabolic enzymes.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Direct stimulator of biliary clearance mechanisms.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
