        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part III: Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">10. What is the molecular target and mechanism of action for Rifampicin?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Inhibition of cell wall production by arresting mycolic acid pathways.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Competitive inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase enzymes.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Inhibition of DNA-dependent RNA polymerase enzyme to block RNA synthesis.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Irreversible disruption of cross-linking structural transpeptidases.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">11. A patient under combination antitubercular therapy complains that their urine has turned an orange-red color. Which medication is responsible?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Rifampicin</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Isoniazid</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Ethambutol</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Erythromycin</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">12. In contrast to Isoniazid, Rifampicin acts on hepatic microsomal enzymes as a:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Direct competitive blocker.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Reversible enzymatic inhibitor.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Non-competitive clearance suppressor.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Hepatic microsomal enzyme inducer.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">13. Which of the following first-line agents is restricted to acting on intracellular organisms only?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Isoniazid</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Ethambutol</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Pyrazinamide</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Rifampicin</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">14. What are the two most common adverse side effects associated with the use of Pyrazinamide?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Hepatotoxicity and hyperuricemia.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Optic neuritis and retrobulbar damage.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Flu-like syndrome and orange body fluid discoloration.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Protein synthesis breakdown and peripheral neuropathies.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">15. Why is the administration of Ethambutol contraindicated in children?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Because it triggers extreme fluctuations in systemic uric acid values.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> To avoid retrobulbar neuritis/severe optic neuritis, which is difficult to monitor in children.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Due to its inability to cross the pediatric blood-brain barrier effectively.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Because it accelerates hepatic acetylation pathways abnormally.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part IV: Second-Line Agents — Pharmacology of Erythromycin &amp; PAS</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">16. What is the mechanism of action of Erythromycin when utilized as a second-line anti-TB substitution?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> It interferes with mycolic acid integration across cellular membranes.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It blocks the transcription phase via RNA polymerase bindings.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> It competes directly with PABA to limit metabolic folic acids.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> It works to irreversibly inhibit protein synthesis.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">17. Para-amino-salicylic acid (PAS) produces its anti-TB synergy through which two mechanisms?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Inducing microsomal enzymes and neutralizing intracellular acids.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Penetrating the blood-brain barrier and coloring excretions orange.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Competing with PABA to decrease folic acid and blocking hepatic acetylation of Isoniazid.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Inhibiting DNA-dependent RNA polymerases and weakening cell walls.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">PAGE 2 ANSWER KEY 📍 10-C | 11-A | 12-D | 13-C | 14-A | 15-B | 16-D | 17-C</div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <div>Suez University Faculty of Human Medicine — Pharmacology Department</div>
                <div>Page 2 of 3</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

