        <div class="section-title">Part V: Supplementary Advanced Details &amp; Precision Mechanics</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">18. According to "Antituberculosis drugs.pdf", what is the exact cellular distribution profile of Isoniazid (INH) action against mycobacteria?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> It acts exclusively on extracellular organisms located in necrotic cavities.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It is strictly restricted to intracellular organisms within macrophages.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> It is effective in both intracellular and extracellular organisms.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It only targets atypical non-tuberculous environmental mycobacteria.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">19. Following the cascade of events in the INH mechanism flowchart, what is the ultimate biological outcome on the mycobacteria after creating a weak cell wall?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Tuberculocidal effect (killing the bacteria).</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Tuberculostatic effect (reversibly halting growth).</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Spore formation induction.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Acceleration of hepatic acetylation.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">20. Reviewing the pharmacokinetic slides, what is the primary route of elimination specified for Rifampicin?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Direct alveolar exhalation.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Pure renal glomerular filtration.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Biliary excretion.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Sebum and sweat gland secretion.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">21. Aside from turning secretions into an orange-red color, what other classic systemic adverse presentation is explicitly listed under Rifampicin?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Severe hyperuricemia and gouty arthritis.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Flu like symptoms.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Retrobulbar neuritis and visual loss.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Peripheral neurotoxicity due to vitamin depletion.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">22. Based on the lecture definitions, what are the two specific clinical scenarios that necessitate substituting first-line drugs with second-line antitubercular drugs?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Lack of cheap local manufacturing or high lipophilicity.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Failure to pass the blood-brain barrier or pediatric age.</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> In case of failure (resistance) or contraindication.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Development of orange urine or harmless flu symptoms.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">23. What is the direct consequence of Para-amino-salicylic acid (PAS) blocking the hepatic acetylation of Isoniazid?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> It increases its free level in the blood.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It accelerates its clearance via biliary pathways.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> It completely neutralizes its tuberculocidal efficacy.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It converts it into a hydrophilic compound.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">24. Which of the following drugs is described in the presentation as a lipophilic agent that can pass the blood-brain barrier?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Pyrazinamide</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Para-amino-salicylic acid</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Rifampicin and Isoniazid (INH).</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Ethambutol and Erythromycin.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

